    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "Mayor John Whitmire. We are joined by the honorable John Whitmire Mayor of the city of Houston. Mr. Mayor, welcome back to our show, sir. Good to have you. Thank you. Your class act, by the way. Look, if I haven't said that before, allow me to say that now you are a class act. I listened to what you said. about Sylvester Turner on his passing. And I thought, you know, it would have been really easy to complain about the previous administration because you're facing this $300 million budget deficit that the previous administration really didn't do anything about. But you didn't mention it at all. You kept everything on the high road. But now that you have to have a hiring freeze, it kind of makes me wonder, you know, what you're thinking here as far as going forward. Hiring freeze, I guess, is a good start. But does the city of Houston need to get a doge like where we're seeing at the state and the federal level? Do you think that maybe you could find $300 million worth of waste in the city of Houston or duplication in the city of Houston that could maybe make up that budget deficit? Well, that's a lot of how much time you got. Of course, I think we can and not all that we must. And we started last May. In fact, we started today. I got there. I grew up in Houston, been in state government for 50 years. So I've worked with the city of Houston. I know of their waste and duplication and quite frankly corruption and conflicts of interest. So we started last January when I was sworn in, aware of too much government in the city of Houston. You can drive by and see construction projects and They're just not getting done because they're not efficient. So we talked to department heads. I put a new team in place. And then in May, we realized we needed outside professional review of city government. So we hired Ernst and Young. They turned in the report three weeks ago. The city has more employees per capita than any other major city. So we're in the process of looking at a retirement recommendation, strong recommendation for some. But in the meantime, we've ordered a freeze on employment, not excluding fire and police. 60% of our budget, by the way, is fire and police. So we have to be smart. We have to have a method. And quite frankly, doge is a whole other operation. Irish's methodical thought out. The morale is good in fire and police. The morale, quite frankly, among city employees is good. I'll go step further. I think the morale of Houstonians is good. We are going in the right direction. But I was left with some major, major challenges. And of course, bringing them up at the news of the loss of Sveta Turner was not the appropriate time. But I did inherit a mess and I'm not shy about explaining that.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
